<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>The clause in plain terms</h2>
<p>Look in the conditions section of almost any South Dakota homeowners, farm or commercial property policy and you will find a paragraph called <em>Appraisal</em>. Its wording varies by carrier, but the bones are the same: if you and the insurer cannot agree on the amount of loss, either side can demand appraisal in writing. Each side picks an appraiser. The two appraisers pick an umpire. A written agreement signed by any two of the three sets the amount.</p>
<p>That structure is why appraisal can break a stalemate that months of emails could not. It is also why the person you name matters. The other side’s appraiser will be experienced. Yours should be too.</p>
<h2>What appraisal clause representation includes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Deadline check on day one.</strong> Some policy forms give each side 20 days to name an appraiser after a demand; others give no number at all. We read your form and the demand letter before anything else.</li>
<li><strong>A fresh inspection.</strong> We walk, measure and photograph the property ourselves. Editing the carrier’s estimate is not the same as valuing the loss.</li>
<li><strong>A line-item valuation</strong> priced for your part of the state. Labor and material costs in Rapid City, Sioux Falls and a ranch outside Philip are not the same, and the estimate should show it.</li>
<li><strong>Working sessions with the carrier’s appraiser</strong> to narrow the disagreement item by item. Most of the gap usually closes here.</li>
<li><strong>Umpire selection and presentation.</strong> If items remain, we help choose a qualified, neutral umpire and present the open items with photos, measurements and pricing support.</li>
<li><strong>Award review.</strong> Before signatures, we make sure the award states replacement cost and actual cash value clearly, so payment and depreciation recovery are not argued all over again.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">The limit of appraisal</b>An appraisal panel decides how much a covered loss costs. It does not decide coverage, interpret exclusions or award damages for bad faith. If the claim was denied outright, a South Dakota attorney is usually the first call.</div>
<h2>When representation is the right fit</h2>
<p>Appraisal works well when the carrier has accepted at least part of the claim and the fight is about scope or price: how many roof slopes were hit, whether the siding can be matched, what a pole barn’s steel panels cost to replace this year. It works poorly when the real argument is wear and tear, late notice, or whether a policy exclusion applies.</p>
<h2>How we keep numbers credible</h2>
<p>We would rather tell you an honest number than a flattering one. Umpires discount padded estimates quickly, and once an appraiser loses credibility, every line suffers. A supported number moves an award. An inflated one rarely does.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can my contractor be my appraiser?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Most appraisal clauses call for a competent and independent or disinterested appraiser. Someone who stands to earn the repair contract may not meet that standard, and using them can give the other side grounds to challenge the award.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who pays for appraisal?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Under most policies each side pays its own appraiser and the two sides split the umpire’s fee and other shared costs. Check your policy, because wording varies.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can the insurer refuse a proper appraisal demand?</summary><div class="ans"><p>When the policy has an appraisal clause and the dispute is about the amount of loss, the carrier generally must take part. Coverage disputes are different and may fall outside appraisal.</p></div></details></div>
